                <h2>Step 1: Calculate a Balance Baseline for Each Account</h2>
<p>A single fixed alert threshold rarely works for every advertising account. Charging schedules, payment methods, campaign budgets, and approval statuses differ, so the same balance may trigger an alert too late in one account and too often in another.</p>
<p>Build a budget baseline for each account:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Average daily spend:</strong> Use the average spend from the last 7 or 14 days, while noting differences between weekdays, weekends, and promotional periods.</li>
<li><strong>Days of runway:</strong> Divide spendable balance by average daily spend rather than looking only at the remaining account balance.</li>
<li><strong>Scaling buffer:</strong> Reserve an additional two to three days of expected spend before increasing budgets or duplicating winning creatives. Adjust this based on cash flow and recharge processing times.</li>
<li><strong>Minimum safety level:</strong> Cover at least one complete charging cycle, with extra room for payment delays, currency conversion, and fees.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, a utility app plans to increase its daily budget from $500 to $800. If its spendable balance is only $900, a fixed threshold may not provide enough runway for the full scaling period. The team should recharge the account before changing the budget.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Configure Tiered Balance Alerts</h2>
<h3>Observation Alert</h3>
<p>Send a standard notification when the balance remains above the normal safety level. Include the account, currency, estimated runway, and responsible owner. This stage is mainly for monitoring and record-keeping.</p>
<h3>Warning Alert</h3>
<p>Trigger a warning when the estimated runway falls below the planned campaign duration. The alert should not simply say “Low balance.” Include the current spendable balance, seven-day average spend, estimated days of runway, upcoming budget changes, and the recommended recharge amount.</p>
<h3>Urgent Alert</h3>
<p>Issue an immediate warning when the balance cannot cover the next charging cycle or a major budget increase is planned. The owner must verify payment status, expected settlement time, and whether scaling should be paused to prevent the platform from restricting delivery at the next deduction.</p>
<p>Thresholds should vary by account. Mature accounts with stable spend can be managed primarily by remaining runway. New accounts or accounts with volatile budgets should use alerts based on both balance and consumption speed.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Run a Pre-Scaling Interruption Check</h2>
<p>Before a large recharge or budget increase, do not rely only on the balance displayed in the platform interface.</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Verify the recharge amount, currency, payer, and settlement status to identify incomplete or duplicate payments.</li>
<li>[ ] Review spendable balance rather than total deposits, excluding frozen, pending, or delayed amounts.</li>
<li>[ ] Check campaign, ad set, and shared budgets to confirm that available funds can support the increased spend.</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm that the payment method remains valid, with no expired card, insufficient limit, failed verification, or disabled automatic payments.</li>
<li>[ ] Check whether minimum spending requirements may prevent stable delivery despite having sufficient funds.</li>
<li>[ ] Record the expected posting time and schedule alerts before the next deduction.</li>
</ul>
<p>For teams managing multiple ad accounts, distinguish between the total wallet balance, each account’s balance, and individual campaign budgets. A healthy overall balance does not mean that every account has enough spendable funds.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Verify Charging After the Recharge Lands</h2>
<p>Once the recharge appears in the account, continue monitoring instead of immediately committing all funds to a scale-up. Confirm that the balance declines as expected, active campaigns continue spending, and new budget levels are accepted.</p>
<p>Pause further budget increases if the payment is still pending, spending deviates materially from expectations, campaigns suddenly stop consuming, or the account’s review status changes. Document these issues in the account record so team members do not repeat the same assessment.</p>
<h3>Simplified Workflow</h3>
<ol>
<li>The system triggers a balance alert.</li>
<li>The media buyer reviews daily spend and estimated runway.</li>
<li>Finance verifies the currency, payment status, and settlement cycle.</li>
<li>The campaign owner chooses whether to recharge, adjust budgets, or delay scaling.</li>
<li>The team verifies charging and delivery after the funds are credited.</li>
<li>Owners review thresholds weekly and adjust them for anomalies or seasonal spending changes.</li>
</ol>
<p>An effective balance alert does more than send notifications. It ensures that the right person knows what needs to be done before delivery is interrupted, and provides a clear verification step after the issue is resolved.</p>
